Interpretation (evidence-based):
  - Register footprint UNCHANGED (48), occupancy essentially unchanged.
  - Shared-memory pipe (L1/TEX 84.9%) is the dominant bottleneck; FMA pipe
    only 38%, so this kernel is shared-memory-latency/bandwidth bound, not
    compute bound.
  - Shared-load instruction count (6,291,456) is 24x the shared-store count
    and 24x the global-load count: the compute inner loop re-issues Bs loads
    for every (i,j) accumulator step.
  - Shared-load bank conflicts (4,383,380) persist at ~70% of shared-load
    instructions -> padding is NOT the fix; the load pattern itself is.
  - Wall-clock win (-19.46%) comes from fewer/vectorized GLOBAL loads
    (262,144 global-ld insts) and reduced instruction count, not from
    conflict reduction.

NEXT EXPERIMENT (from this profile):
  Hoist the thread's B column segment out of the i-loop into a small
  register array (b_reg[THREAD_TILE_N]) loaded once per k step.
  Expected: shared-load instructions drop 20 -> 8 per (thread,k) step
  (~60% fewer), proportionally fewer bank conflicts, lower L1/TEX pressure.
  NOT in scope: float4 C writeback (rejected), BK+1 padding (rejected).
