EXPERIMENT RESULT (2026-08-13): B-column register hoist (manual source change)
  Attempted: hoist Bs[k][tx*4+j] into b_reg[4] loaded once per k step.
  Result:     NO-OP. ncu metrics byte-identical before/after the edit:
              48 regs, 52,701,184 SASS insts, 6,291,456 shared-ld, 262,144 shared-st,
              SM 59.70-60.02%, warps 68.53-68.70%.
  Conclusion: nvcc -O3 ALREADY hoists the B-column loads into registers.
              Manual source hoist changes nothing. Source REVERTED to accepted
              baseline; rebuild verified (max_rel=0.000657, PASS).
  Next:       Shared-load traffic is compiler-optimal for this tile shape.
              The 6.29M shared loads = 4 (A row) + 4 (B col) per (thread,k) step
              is the theoretical floor for THREAD_TILE=4x4 scalar loads.
              Larger register tile (THREAD_TILE_M/N) or wider LDS (float4 shared
              loads) would be the next lever, but changes the whole kernel shape.

=== EXPERIMENT: THREAD_TILE_M 4->8 (register-tile enlargement) ===
Date: 2026-08-13
Status: REVERTED (regression)

Motivation: SASS cuobjdump analysis showed compiler already emits LDS.128 (B) + LDS.64 (A)
fully unrolled; shared loads already max-vectorized at 4x4 tile. Only remaining lever to cut
shared-load-per-FMA is larger register tile.

Change: THREAD_TILE_M 4->8 (THREAD_TILE_N stayed 4), generalized A-tile loader loop.
B load unchanged (tile cols still 64). C_local 16 -> 32 accumulators.

First build FAILED correctness: 32758/65536 outliers (max_rel=1.0). Root cause: A-tile loader
only covered 64/128 rows (256 threads x one float4 = 1024 floats vs 2048 needed). Fixed with
a_units_per_thread=2 loop. Correctness then PASS all paths + 9 shapes (max_rel=0.000657).

Bench (30-sample, noisy machine):
  TILE8: median=0.318432 ms  min=0.309728  max=0.37152
  BASE : median=0.286096 ms
  => -11.3% SLOWER. Uniform regression (quiet min 0.3097 > baseline median).

ncu (timed launch, register_tiled_gemm<1,1>):
  grid (16,8,1) = 128 blocks (was 16x16=256)
  registers/thread: 72 (was 48)
  max warps/active cycle: 50% (was 68.7%)
  L1TEX throughput: 73.37% (was 84.90%) -- shared-load bottleneck REDUCED as predicted
  SM throughput: 52.21%
  warp latency/inst issued: 10.03 cycles

Verdict: Bigger tile cut shared-load pressure (L1TEX 84.9->73.4) BUT register pressure
(48->72) dropped occupancy (68.7->50%), netting -11%. Occupancy-limited on SM120.
KEEP/REVERT protocol => REVERTED. Working tree restored to byte-identical accepted baseline.
